// 05 · ownership — one value, one owner, no second checker
//
// Ownership in Mentl is not a separate type system bolted on the side. It is
// an effect: passing a value `own` into a function CONSUMES it; passing `ref`
// borrows it. The same row machinery that proves `Pure` proves single-use, so
// there is no borrow checker to satisfy separately — the row decides.
//
// And you rarely write the marker. Inference reads the use count: a value used
// once and not aliased is consumed; a value only read is borrowed. You annotate
// `own` or `ref` only to state intent at a boundary, the way you annotate a
// type only where it matters. The bar is Hylo's: if you find yourself thinking
// about ownership, the inference failed.
//
// Run it:   mentl run  lib/tutorial/05-ownership.mn   ← prints 14 (byte length, twice)
// Read it:  mentl lib/tutorial/05-ownership.mn:21:11  ← the cursor on a param; own/ref in the ring
import io

// ref — borrow. `length` reads its argument and gives it back; the caller can
// use the string again afterward. Inference would reach `ref` here on its own;
// the marker just says so out loud.
fn length(ref s) = byte_len(s)

// Two borrows of the same value, both fine — reading never consumes.
fn measure() = {
  let greeting = "octopus"
  let a = length(greeting)
  let b = length(greeting)
  a + b
}

// own — consume. `archive` takes ownership and hands the value on (returning
// an owned value is the transfer); after the call the value is gone from the
// caller, and any later reference to it there is a compile-time error
// (E_OwnershipViolation). The medium proves single-use the same way it proves
// everything else — and it narrates the inverse: an `own` parameter a body
// only READS is a claim without a consume, and the ring says so.
fn archive(own data) = data

// Branches are ALTERNATIVES, not both-run, so consuming `x` in each arm of an
// if is sound: exactly one arm runs, so `x` is consumed exactly once. The
// affine ledger scopes per branch and knows this. (Consume it twice in the same
// path — `archive(x)` then `archive(x)` — and that is the error it catches.)
fn choose(own x) = if byte_len("flag") > 3 { archive(x) } else { archive(x) }

fn main() = measure() |> int_to_str |> print_string
